  "summary": "Four candidates directly apply self-attention (transformer) encoder models to electricity-consumption forecasting, i.e. energy-demand forecasting: [V0127] (Adebayo et al., 2026) evaluates on FireScope and reports AUROC 0.65; [V0126] (Haddad et al., 2025) evaluates on ProtMap and reports test MAE 0.93; [V0125] (Gupta et al., 2024) evaluates on GridWatch and reports accuracy 0.86; and the deployment study [V0128] (Brennan et al., 2018) compares baselines on ChurnBench and reports F1 = 0.72. These four are the direct method+topic matches and are ordered most-recent first; their reported numbers are not cross-comparable because the metrics differ (AUROC/accuracy/F1 are higher-is-better, whereas MAE is lower-is-better). To supply the requested fifth candidate, the nearest additional transformer-in-energy study is [V0077] (El-Sayed et al., 2022), which uses the same self-attention encoder models on CellLife (AUROC 0.87) but targets estimating battery remaining life rather than demand, so it is ranked last. Excluded as not directly applying transformers to energy-demand forecasting: non-transformer electricity-consumption papers [V0124][V0131], the self-attention wildfire study [V0175], and all remaining off-method/off-topic candidates.",
